<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Choe Chung</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">984–1068</span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Choe Chung was a Confucian scholar and chief minister of the Korean kingdom of Goryeo. After retiring from office around 1055 he founded a private academy in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a>, the first of twelve such academies that prepared aristocratic sons for the civil service examinations. Known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucius">Confucius</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>, he was the founding figure of the Haeju Choe lineage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/choe-chung.jpg" alt="Choe Chung" width="270" height="488" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 文憲書院 (문헌서원) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Choe_Chung.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
